counterexample:
`Context-dependent`. Low-level library code or platform abstraction boundaries (like standard library OS-specific file modules) where platform checks are unavoidable; high-level module definitions gating platform-specific drivers (e.g., `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")] mod win;`); and provider/mock implementations cleanly separated into distinct modules behind a clear interface.
```rust
trait PaymentProvider {
    fn pay(&self);
}
// Implementations are cleanly separated into distinct platform/mock modules
```

fix:
Group each variant in a cohesive module or implementation behind an appropriate interface or trait, and conditionally compile the entire module or implementation at the compilation or linker boundary rather than inline statements. For hardware variants, encapsulate target dependencies under a Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL) trait and select the active implementation during system initialization. Keep common business logic free of scattered platform switches.
```rust
trait Board {
    fn init_pinout(&self);
    fn read_sensors(&self);
}
```
